Parameter-efficient fine-tuning has become the default route to adapting multimodal models, yet the literature presents it as a problem of \emph{method selection}: papers propose alternative adapters and report that theirs performs best. This critical review argues that the framing obscures the problem's actual structure: two decisions, orthogonal and usually conflated. The first is \emph{where} adaptive capacity is placed — in the visual encoder, in the projector that couples the modalities, in the language backbone, or across the full stack — a choice governed by model architecture and data volume. The second is \emph{how} a fixed capacity budget is divided among the modules chosen by the first, a choice governed by the metric one cares about. Conflating the two is not merely presentational: it is why much of the published evidence cannot be compared. The argument rests on instances checkable against their sources, drawn from published work rather than a systematic sample: reported numbers for the same base model that differ because an evaluation subset is unstated; a protocol change that moves a model's score from 36.7\% to 2.6\%; and "parameter count" used as a proxy for a budget that different papers divide differently and compose from different components. I close with a twelve-item reporting checklist intended to make adaptation results comparable, and argue that the field's next contribution is better reporting, not another adapter.
